data: {"id":"chatcmpl-20261017221728140595128c955d568OEKt6bHK","created":1792275449,"object":"chat.completion.chunk","model":"glm-5.3-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_c24651b482e046d7a997177e","index":0,"type":"function","function":{"name":"write","arguments":"{\"content\":\"PROOF CERTIFICATE FOR THE sort_list OBLIGATIONS\\n================================================\\n\\nCertificate for: obligations.txt (OBL-1 .. OBL-7)\\nReferences:      logic.txt (axioms A1-A6, L1-L3, definitions 2.3, rules of 2),\\n                 sort.py (implementation, used only by OBL-8 / trusted item T4).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n1. CERTIFICATE FORMAT (for the checker)\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nEach proof is a finite sequence of steps. A step has the form\\n\\n    <id>.  <context> |- <formula>        [by <rule>(<refs>)]\\n\\nRules (all standard natural deduction, per logic.txt Section 2):\\n\\n    AX a      instance of axiom a (A1-A6 or L1-L3)\\n    DEF f     unfold one defining equation of f (logic.txt 2.3); admissible\\n              only after OBL-1 and OBL-2 are proved (trusted item T3)\\n    INST      universal elimination (instantiate a forall)\\n    GEN       universal introduction (generalize over a fresh variable)\\n    ASSUME    implication introduction (add hypothesis to context)\\n    APPLY     implication elimination (modus ponens)\\n    CASE c    case analysis justified by c (trichotomy A3, or the two\\n              branches of an if-then-else in a definition)\\n    IND L3    list induction (axiom L3): base case + step case\\n    IH        use of the induction hypothesis introduced by IND\\n    CONTRA    ex falso: from a contradiction derived in context, any formula\\n    EQ r      equality reasoning: REFL, SYM, TRANS, SUBST (congruence)\\n    ARITH     linear integer arithmetic reasoning from A1-A6 (a derived\\n              decision procedure; its soundness is checked inside T1)\\n\\nChecker duties: every referenced id exists earlier in the file; every rule\\ninstance is well-sorted; the final step of each proof matches the stated\\nobligation verbatim; no step is labeled \\\"sorry\\\". If all checks pass, the\\nobligation is discharged.\\n\\nProof order respects dependencies: termination first (OBL-1, OBL-2), so\\nthat DEF becomes admissible; then the lemmas and main obligations.\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n2. OBL-1  Termination of insert\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGoal:  forall v : Z. forall M : List. terminates(insert(v, M)).\\n\\nProof by IND (L3) on M with P(M) = forall v. terminates(insert(v, M)).\\n\\n  1.1.  terminates(insert(v, nil))\\n          [by DEF insert: insert(v,nil) = cons(v,nil); no recursive calls]\\n  1.2.  |- forall v. terminates(insert(v, nil))            [by GEN(1.1)]\\n          -- base case of IND established.\\n\\n  1.3.  IH: forall v. terminates(insert(v, ys)).\\n  1.4.  insert(v, cons(y, ys))\\n          = if v <= y then cons(v, cons(y, ys))\\n                      else cons(y, insert(v, ys))        [by DEF insert]\\n  1.5.  len(ys) = len(cons(y, ys)) - 1                    [by DEF len, ARITH]\\n  1.6.  len(ys) < len(cons(y, ys))                        [by 1.5, A1, A4]\\n          -- measure decreases; well-foundedness of < on Z >= 0 from A1-A4.\\n  1.7.  Case v <= y: result is cons(v, cons(y, ys)); no recursive call,\\n        hence terminates.                                 [by 1.4, CASE A3]\\n  1.8.  Case v > y: result is cons(y, insert(v, ys));\\n        terminates(insert(v, ys))                         [by IH(1.3), INST]\\n        hence terminates(insert(v, cons(y, ys))).         [by 1.4, 1.8a, APPLY]\\n  1.9.  forall v. terminates(insert(v, cons(y, ys)))\\n          [by 1.7, 1.8, CASE A3, GEN]\\n          -- step case of IND established.\\n\\n  1.10. |- forall M. forall v. terminates(insert(v, M))   [by IND L3 (1.2, 1.9)]\\n\\nOBL-1: PROVED  (final step 1.10).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n3. OBL-2  Termination of sort_list\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGoal:  forall L : List. terminates(sort_list(L)).\\n\\nProof by IND (L3) on L.\\n\\n  2.1.  sort_list(nil) = nil, no recursive calls          [by DEF sort_list]\\n  2.2.  terminates(sort_list(nil))                        [by 2.1]\\n          -- base case.\\n  2.3.  IH: terminates(sort_list(xs)).\\n  2.4.  sort_list(cons(x, xs)) = insert(x, sort_list(xs)) [by DEF sort_list]\\n  2.5.  terminates(sort_list(xs))                         [by IH(2.3)]\\n  2.6.  terminates(insert(x, sort_list(xs)))              [by OBL-1, INST x, sort_list(xs)]\\n  2.7.  terminates(sort_list(cons(x, xs)))                [by 2.4, 2.5, 2.6, APPLY]\\n          -- step case; measure len(xs) < len(cons(x,xs)) as in 1.5-1.6.\\n\\n  2.8.  |- forall L. terminates(sort_list(L))             [by IND L3 (2.2, 2.7)]\\n\\nOBL-2: PROVED  (final step 2.8).  With OBL-1 this discharges request\\nitem (3): the function terminates for all inputs.  From here on, DEF is\\nadmissible for insert and sort_list (trusted item T3).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n4. OBL-5  Well-definedness of nth (supporting)\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGoal:  forall M. forall i. 0 <= i < len(M) -> exists x : Z. nth(M, i) = x.\\n\\nProof by IND (L3) on M.\\n\\n  4.1.  M = nil: len(nil) = 0 [DEF len]; hypotheses 0 <= i and i < 0 are\\n        contradictory                                        [by ARITH, A1]\\n  4.2.  exists x. nth(nil, i) = x                            [by 4.1, CONTRA]\\n          -- base case.\\n  4.3.  IH: forall i. 0 <= i < len(xs) -> exists x. nth(xs, i) = x.\\n  4.4.  h: 0 <= i < len(cons(x, xs)) = 1 + len(xs)           [by DEF len]\\n  4.5.  Case i = 0: nth(cons(x, xs), 0) = x                  [by DEF nth]\\n        witness x.                                           [by 4.5a]\\n  4.6.  Case i > 0: nth(cons(x, xs), i) = nth(xs, i - 1)     [by DEF nth]\\n        0 <= i - 1 < len(xs)                                 [by 4.4, ARITH]\\n        exists x. nth(xs, i - 1) = x                         [by IH(4.3), INST i-1]\\n        hence exists x. nth(cons(x, xs), i) = x              [by 4.6a, EQ SUBST]\\n  4.7.  forall i. 0 <= i < len(cons(x,xs)) -> exists x. nth(cons(x,xs), i) = x\\n                                                             [by 4.5, 4.6, CASE ARITH, GEN]\\n\\n  4.8.  |- forall M. forall i. 0 <= i < len(M) -> exists x. nth(M, i) = x\\n                                                             [by IND L3 (4.2, 4.7)]\\n\\nOBL-5: PROVED  (final step 4.8).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n5. Auxiliary lemmas for sortedness\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nNotation: s(k) abbreviates nth(M, k) when M is clear from context.\\n\\nLEMMA S2 (tail of sorted is sorted):\\n  5.1.  h: sorted(cons(y, ys)); 0 <= i <= j < len(ys).\\n  5.2.  0 <= i+1 <= j+1 < 1 + len(ys) = len(cons(y, ys))     [by ARITH, DEF len]\\n  5.3.  nth(cons(y,ys), i+1) <= nth(cons(y,ys), j+1)\\n          [by h, INST i+1, j+1; 5.2 guards the range]\\n  5.4.  nth(ys, i) <= nth(ys, j)                             [by 5.3, DEF nth, EQ]\\n  5.5.  |- sorted(cons(y, ys)) -> sorted(ys)\\n          [by 5.1-5.4, ASSUME, GEN]\\n\\nLEMMA S1 (head is a lower bound):\\n  5.6.  h: sorted(cons(y, ys)); k with 0 <= k < len(cons(y, ys)).\\n  5.7.  Case k = 0: y <= y                                   [by A6, EQ REFL]\\n  5.8.  Case k > 0: y = nth(cons(y,ys), 0) <= nth(cons(y,ys), k)\\n          [by h, INST 0, k; ARITH gives 0 <= 0 <= k < len; DEF nth]\\n  5.9.  |- sorted(cons(y,ys)) -> forall k. 0 <= k < len(cons(y,ys)) -> y <= nth(cons(y,ys), k)\\n          [by 5.7, 5.8, CASE ARITH, GEN, ASSUME]\\n\\nLEMMA S3 (cons introduction):\\n  Goal: sorted(M) /\\\\ (forall k. 0 <= k < len(M) -> y <= nth(M,k))\\n        -> sorted(cons(y, M)).\\n  5.10. h1: sorted(M); h2: forall k. 0 <= k < len(M) -> y <= nth(M,k);\\n        0 <= i <= j < len(cons(y, M)) = 1 + len(M)           [by DEF len]\\n  5.11. Case i = 0, j = 0: nth(cons(y,M),0) <= nth(cons(y,M),0)\\n                                                             [by A6, EQ REFL]\\n  5.12. Case i = 0, j > 0: nth(cons(y,M), j) = nth(M, j-1)   [by DEF nth]\\n        y <= nth(M, j-1)   (0 <= j-1 < len(M) by ARITH)      [by h2, INST j-1]\\n        hence nth(cons(y,M), 0) <= nth(cons(y,M), j)         [by DEF nth, 5.12a, TRANS]\\n  5.13. Case i > 0 (so j > 0):\\n        nth(cons(y,M), i) = nth(M, i-1),\\n        nth(cons(y,M), j) = nth(M, j-1)                      [by DEF nth]\\n        0 <= i-1 <= j-1 < len(M)                             [by ARITH]\\n        nth(M, i-1) <= nth(M, j-1)                           [by h1, INST i-1, j-1]\\n        hence nth(cons(y,M), i) <= nth(cons(y,M), j)         [by EQ, TRANS]\\n  5.14. |- (sorted(M) /\\\\ (forall k. 0 <= k < len(M) -> y <= nth(M,k)))\\n             -> sorted(cons(y, M))         [by 5.10-5.13, CASE ARITH, GEN, ASSUME]\\n\\nLEMMA S4 (insert preserves lower bounds):\\n  Goal: forall y v. (forall k. 0 <= k < len(M) -> y <= nth(M,k)) /\\\\ y <= v\\n        -> forall k. 0 <= k < len(insert(v,M)) -> y <= nth(insert(v,M), k).\\n  Proof by IND (L3) on M (y, v fixed).\\n  5.15. M = nil: insert(v,nil) = cons(v,nil)                 [by DEF insert]\\n        only k = 0 in range; nth = v >= y                    [by DEF nth, hyp]\\n  5.16. M = cons(x, xs); IH as above for xs.\\n        bound(xs): forall k. 0 <= k < len(xs) -> y <= nth(xs, k):\\n          for such k, k+1 is in range of cons(x,xs), so\\n          y <= nth(cons(x,xs), k+1) = nth(xs, k)             [by hyp, INST k+1, DEF nth]\\n        Case v <= x: insert = cons(v, cons(x,xs))            [by DEF insert]\\n          k = 0: y <= v = nth(cons(v, rest), 0)              [by hyp, DEF nth]\\n          k > 0: nth(cons(v,rest), k) = nth(rest, k-1) >= y  [by hyp, INST k-1, DEF nth]\\n        Case v > x: insert = cons(x, insert(v, xs))          [by DEF insert]\\n          k = 0: y <= x = nth(cons(x, ...), 0)               [by hyp, INST 0, DEF nth]\\n          k > 0: nth(cons(x, insert(v,xs)), k) = nth(insert(v,xs), k-1)\\n                 and 0 <= k-1 < len(insert(v,xs)), so by IH\\n                 y <= nth(insert(v,xs), k-1)                 [by IH, INST k-1, DEF nth]\\n  5.17. |- forall y v. (forall k. 0 <= k < len(M) -> y <= nth(M,k)) /\\\\ y <= v\\n             -> forall k. 0 <= k < len(insert(v,M)) -> y <= nth(insert(v,M), k)\\n                                                             [by IND L3, CASE, GEN]\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n6. LEMMA L3-a and OBL-3  Sortedness\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nLEMMA L3-a:  forall v. forall M. sorted(M) -> sorted(insert(v, M)).\\nProof by IND (L3) on M (v fixed).\\n\\n  6.1.  M = nil: insert(v,nil) = cons(v,nil)                 [by DEF insert]\\n        sorted(nil): vacuous, len(nil) = 0 admits no indices [by DEF len, ARITH, CONTRA]\\n        sorted(cons(v,nil))                                  [by 6.1a, 6.1b, LEMMA S3]\\n          -- base case.\\n  6.2.  M = cons(x, xs); h: sorted(cons(x, xs));\\n        IH: sorted(xs) -> sorted(insert(v, xs)).\\n  6.3.  sorted(xs)                                           [by h, LEMMA S2]\\n  6.4.  sorted(insert(v, xs))                                [by IH, 6.3, APPLY]\\n  6.5.  Case v <= x: insert(v, cons(x,xs)) = cons(v, cons(x,xs))  [by DEF insert]\\n        Apply LEMMA S3 with M := cons(x,xs), y := v:\\n          - sorted(cons(x,xs))                               [h]\\n          - bound: k = 0: v <= x (case); k > 0: v <= x <= nth(xs, k-1)\\n            and x <= nth(xs, k-1) by LEMMA S1 (INST) + 6.3   [TRANS]\\n          hence sorted(cons(v, cons(x,xs)))                  [by LEMMA S3]\\n  6.6.  Case v > x: insert(v, cons(x,xs)) = cons(x, insert(v, xs))  [by DEF insert]\\n        x <= v                                               [by case, A3, A6]\\n        bound(insert(v,xs)) w.r.t. x:\\n          by LEMMA S4 with y := x, v := v, using bound(xs) from\\n          LEMMA S1 + 6.3 (as in 5.16) and x <= v             [by LEMMA S4]\\n        Apply LEMMA S3 with M := insert(v,xs), y := x:\\n          sorted(insert(v,xs)) (6.4) and the bound above\\n          hence sorted(cons(x, insert(v, xs)))               [by 6.4, 6.6a, LEMMA S3]\\n  6.7.  sorted(insert(v, cons(x, xs)))                       [by 6.5, 6.6, CASE A3]\\n  6.8.  |- forall v. forall M. sorted(M) -> sorted(insert(v, M))\\n                                                             [by IND L3 (6.1, 6.7), GEN]\\n\\nLEMMA L3-a: PROVED (final step 6.8).\\n\\nOBL-3 (request item (1)):  forall L. sorted(sort_list(L)).\\nProof by IND (L3) on L.\\n\\n  6.9.  L = nil: sort_list(nil) = nil                        [by DEF sort_list]\\n        sorted(nil) as in 6.1b                               [by ARITH, CONTRA]\\n          -- base case.\\n  6.10. L = cons(x, xs); IH: sorted(sort_list(xs)).\\n        sort_list(cons(x, xs)) = insert(x, sort_list(xs))    [by DEF sort_list]\\n        sorted(insert(x, sort_list(xs)))                     [by L3-a(6.8) INST, IH, APPLY]\\n  6.11. |- forall L. sorted(sort_list(L))                    [by IND L3 (6.9, 6.10)]\\n\\nOBL-3: PROVED  (final step 6.11).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n7. OBL-6  Count decomposition lemmas (supporting)\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n  7.1.  forall v. count(nil, v) = 0                          [by DEF count (nil eq)]\\n  7.2.  forall x xs v.\\n          count(cons(x, xs), v)\\n            = (if x = v then 1 else 0) + count(xs, v)        [by DEF count (cons eq)]\\n  7.3.  The two summands in 7.2 are terms of sort Z; reassociation and\\n        reordering are justified by A5 (associativity, commutativity,\\n        unit) via ARITH.                                     [by A5, ARITH]\\n\\nOBL-6: PROVED  (steps 7.1-7.3; direct definitional instances, no induction).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n8. LEMMA L4-a and OBL-4  Permutation\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRecall: permutation(L, M) =def forall v : Z. count(L, v) = count(M, v).\\n\\nLEMMA L4-a:  forall v. forall M. permutation(insert(v, M), cons(v, M)).\\nProof by IND (L3) on M (v fixed).  Fix arbitrary w : Z throughout.\\n\\n  8.1.  M = nil: insert(v, nil) = cons(v, nil)               [by DEF insert]\\n        count(cons(v,nil), w) = count(cons(v,nil), w)        [by EQ REFL]\\n          -- base case; GEN over w.\\n  8.2.  M = cons(y, ys); IH: forall w. count(insert(v,ys), w) = count(cons(v,ys), w).\\n        Unfold count twice on both sides                     [by DEF count, OBL-6.2]:\\n        LHS goal:  count(insert(v, cons(y,ys)), w)\\n        RHS goal:  count(cons(v, cons(y,ys)), w)\\n                 = (if v = w then 1 else 0)\\n                   + ((if y = w then 1 else 0) + count(ys, w))\\n  8.3.  Case v <= y: insert(v, cons(y,ys)) = cons(v, cons(y,ys))   [by DEF insert]\\n        LHS = (if v=w then 1 else 0) + ((if y=w then 1 else 0) + count(ys,w))\\n                                                             [by DEF count]\\n        LHS = RHS                                            [by EQ REFL]\\n  8.4.  Case v > y: insert(v, cons(y,ys)) = cons(y, insert(v,ys))  [by DEF insert]\\n        LHS = (if y=w then 1 else 0) + count(insert(v,ys), w)      [by DEF count]\\n            = (if y=w then 1 else 0) + count(cons(v,ys), w)        [by IH, INST w, EQ SUBST]\\n            = (if y=w then 1 else 0) + ((if v=w then 1 else 0) + c