tated for finite lists.)\\n    - Elements are INTEGERS of sort Z, with the standard order and\\n      addition (axioms A1-A6). Python ints are arbitrary precision,\\n      so no overflow bound is needed or assumed; this matches the\\n      model. Bools (True/False) happen to work in Python but are not\\n      part of the declared domain.\\n    - Purity: sort_list copies its input (sort.py line 3) and mutates\\n      only its private copy. The specification is extensional and\\n      assumes no aliasing effects, no concurrency, and no\\n      side-effecting inputs (e.g., list subclasses with overridden\\n      __getitem__ are out of scope).\\n    - Length: no logical bound on list length; the theorem is for all\\n      finite lists. Empirical coverage is bounded (Section 5).\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n3. EXPLICIT EXCLUSIONS (NOT VERIFIED, NOT CLAIMED)\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    - Non-integer element types: floats (NaN breaks total order:\\n      NaN comparisons are false, violating trichotomy A3), strings,\\n      mixed types, Decimal/Fraction, None. Behavior for these is\\n      unspecified by the verification.\\n    - Stability: permutation is multiset equality via counts, which\\n      does not capture the relative order of equal elements.\\n      Insertion sort happens to be stable, but stability is NOT part\\n      of the specification and NOT verified.\\n    - Complexity and resources: termination is proved, but no\\n      time/memory bound is formalized. (Insertion sort is O(n^2)\\n      comparisons / O(n) aux space; that is an implementation\\n      property, not an obligation.)\\n    - Concurrency, reentrancy, and interpreter interrupts are outside\\n      the semantic model.\\n    - Python's sorted() used as the test oracle in conformance runs is\\n      itself trusted, not verified.\\n    - No claims about code not in sort.py.\\n\\n-------------------